A TRAILING `continue` SILENTLY DELETES THE REST OF THE ENCLOSING BLOCK -- IN
FOR AND DO. WhilE WAS FIXED 2026-08-24; ITS TWO SIBLINGS WERE NOT.

WHAT YOU JUST SAW. Three arms, identical shape: a loop whose last executed
statement is `continue`, then one statement after the loop. All three arms
print "arm entered". All three loops run three passes. Only WhilE reaches the
statement after its loop. FOR and DO print NOTHING after their loop -- not the
assignment, not the report, nothing -- and the run still exits 0.

/!\ THE ARM-ENTERED AND PASS MARKERS ARE THE ANTI-VACUITY CONTROL AND ARE NOT
DECORATION. Without them, "FOR printed nothing" is indistinguishable from "the
FOR arm never ran", which is a completely different defect. The markers prove
the loop ran correctly and the TAIL was eaten.

THE MECHANISM, and it is Tony's own, written when he fixed WhilE (b88f33d):
when the last iteration hits a continue, the loop's `result` is still the
continue SENTINEL, which then propagates to the ENCLOSING block as a continue
and pooches it. One channel carrying two meanings -- "skip this iteration" and
"this is the block's value" -- and the second meaning leaking across a boundary
the first was never meant to cross. His word on the WhilE version: NOT AN
OPTIONAL FIX.

